ate_neutral":0.6667,"def_epa_per_play":0.1965},{"week":8,"opponent":"GB","home":false,"pf":19.0,"pa":27.0,"off_snaps":70,"epa_per_play":-0.1188,"pass_rate":0.6714,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6296,"def_epa_per_play":0.06541},{"week":9,"opponent":"DEN","home":true,"pf":21.0,"pa":26.0,"off_snaps":62,"epa_per_play":0.1055,"pass_rate":0.6774,"pass_rate_neutral":0.56,"def_epa_per_play":0.05232},{"week":10,"opponent":"NO","home":false,"pf":20.0,"pa":17.0,"off_snaps":65,"epa_per_play":-0.0506,"pass_rate":0.5077,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5263,"def_epa_per_play":-0.2251},{"week":11,"opponent":"BAL","home":true,"pf":35.0,"pa":33.0,"off_snaps":67,"epa_per_play":0.0604,"pass_rate":0.4925,"pass_rate_neutral":0.449,"def_epa_per_play":0.04635}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2025,"team":"IND","distance":0.7898,"shared":12,"play_caller":"Shane Steichen"},{"season":2023,"team":"BUF","distance":0.7945,"shared":12,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"CLE","distance":0.8333,"shared":12,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"TB","distance":0.8817,"shared":12,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2024,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.8977,"shared":12,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2022,"team":"NE","distance":0.3246,"shared":4,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"MIN","distance":0.3374,"shared":4,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"KC","distance":0.4161,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Steve Spagnuolo"},{"season":2025,"team":"SF","distance":0.4452,"shared":4,"play_caller":"Robert Saleh"},{"season":2023,"team":"NE","distance":0.4826,"shared":4,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Panthers are 5-5 through week 11 of the 2026 season and have been outscored 269 to 254. The offense ranks 10th in EPA per play and the defense 16th in EPA allowed, so neither side has stood out much yet. What sets them apart is the highest RPO rate and the lowest eight man box rate in the league.","offense":"The Panthers offense has been well above average by EPA per play so far. They have thrown more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score has not called for it. Schematically, they stand out for the highest RPO rate and the fewest backs in the backfield in the league. Bryce Young has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Tetairoa McMillan, who has drawn 25% of the targets.","defense":"The Panthers defense has been about average by EPA allowed per play so far. Its strongest area has been explosive play rate allowed, where it ranks 8th, and its weakest has been sack rate, where it ranks 31st. Princely Umanmielen leads the team with 4.5 sacks, and Jaelan Phillips has added 3.5, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they have the lowest eight man box rate in the league.","special":"The Panthers have been one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 67% of their short-yardage chances so far. Their kickers have made 89% of field goals, which is a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 20% of the money on the books. Their running backs were among the youngest in the league.","players":"Bryce Young has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 2,651 yards. Tetairoa McMillan has been the clear top target with 25% of the looks and 731 receiving yards. Chuba Hubbard has handled most of the work in the backfield with 79% of the carries. On defense, Princely Umanmielen leads the team with 4.5 sacks and Devin Lloyd has picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Dave Canales is calling the offense as head coach and Ejiro Evero is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2025 Colts under Shane Steichen and the 2023 Bills. Their defense looks most like the 2022 Patriots and the 2022 Vikings.","trends":"The offense went from 26th in EPA per play in 2025 to 10th so far this season, a big step forward. The defense moved from 23rd in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 16th so far this season, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was neutral-script pass rate, which went from 51% to 59%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}